          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#details"} Details
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesNonConvergenceOfThePerturbationSeries"} Non-convergence of the perturbation series
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#linfinity_algebra_structure"} L-infinity algebra structure
          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              “God does not do perturbation theory, perturbation theory is what we do because we don’t know any better.” &amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Duff+interview+at+M-Theory-Mathematics+2020"}> Duff 2020
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            What is called
            %em perturbative quantum field theory
            (pQFT) is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            (between
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation"} perturbation
            of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"}> free field theory
            ” where no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            is assumed to take place (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"}> perturbation theory
            ”). This is meant to be an approximation to the actual
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            and/or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological invariance
            and most of the “quantum field theory” in the literature is tacitly understood to be perturbative.
          %p#pQFTAsInfinitesimalThickenedPoint
            Hence pQFT studies the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
            (also called the
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+neighbourhood"} formal neighbourhood
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+quantum+field+theories"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theories"} classical field theories
            in the parameter space of all quantum field theories, the rest of the space being the realm of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+QFT"} non-perturbative QFT
          %p
            Mathematically this means that the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            in pQFT (typically:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            or “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrices"}> S-matrices
            ” encoded by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            which measures the strength of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            (as well as in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            are supposed to be not formal power series algebras, but
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/NonPerturbativeParameterSpace-230613.jpg", :width => "360"}/
          %p
            While just describing a tiny (infinitesimal) part of QFT, perturbative QFT is way better understood than full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+QFT"} non-perturbative QFT
            (which remains ill-understood to the extent that it has been named a
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "mass+gap#ReferencesMassGapProblem"} Millennium Problem
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesForInverseOfCouplingArgument"} below
          %p
            The key object of perturbative QFT is the perturbative
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+matrix"} scattering matrix
            which expresses, as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in the ratio of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitude"} probability amplitude
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering"} scattering
            processes, namely of processes where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free fields
            in a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} state
            come in from the far past, interact and hence scatter off each other, and then go off in some other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            into the far future. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+cross+sections"} scattering cross sections
            thus defined are the quantities which may be directly measured in scattering
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            accelerator.
          %p
            The perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            turns out to have an expression as a sum over separate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            for elementary processes labeled by
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition+principle"} superposition principle
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitude"} probability amplitude
            for a specific outcome is the sum over the probability amplitudes of all the possible processes that can contribute to this outcome.
          %p
            For all interesting
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theories"} interacting field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+matrix"} scattering matrix
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            necessarily has
            %em vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
            (
            %a{:href => "#Dyson52"}> Dyson 52
            ). If it is assumed that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+series"} Taylor series
            of an actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            given by the actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            that is being approximated, then this means that it is at least an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic series
            (by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "asymptotic+series#TaylorSeriesOfSmoothFunctionIsAsymptoticSeries"} this example
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+orders"} loop orders
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            turns out to be in agreement with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            to good precision.
          %p
            (There are however known
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            which are not captured in perturbation theory, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons+in+QCD"} instantons in QCD
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resurgence+theory"} resurgence theory
            one tries to identify these from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic
            nature of the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
          %p
            A key step in the construction of perturbative quantum field theory is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            of the point interactions. This comes about because given
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian+density"} local Lagrangian density
                defining the nature of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
                and their
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
                (generally:
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+state"} Hadamard state
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                to be perturbed about
          %p
            it turns out that the construction of the perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian+density"} local Lagrangian density
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            of the interaction, which is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distribution"} operator-valued distribution
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensions+of+distributions"} extensions of distributions
          %p
            Historically, perturbative quantum field theory as originally conceived informally by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"} Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
            in the 1940s, had been notorious for the mysterious conceptual nature of its mathematical principles (“divergences”). The mathematically rigorous formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            (“removal of
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-divergences"} UV-divergences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            was established by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"} Epstein-Glaser 73
            %a{:href => "#BogoliubovShirkov59"} Bogoliubov-Shirkov 59
            and
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Stueckelberg51"} Stückelberg 51
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#VeloWightman76"} Velo-Wightman 76
          %p
            The correct definition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatic limit
            (“removal of IR divergencies”) was understood in
            %a{:href => "#IlinSlavnov78"} Il’in-Slavnov 78
            and eventually developed by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DuetschFredenhagen01"} Dütsch-Fredenhagen 01
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiDuetschFredenhagen09"} Brunetti-DütschFredenhagen 09
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+rules"} Feynman rules
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensional+regularization"} dimensional regularization
            for computation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            was achieved in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Keller10"} Keller 10 (IV.12)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DuetschFredenhagenKellerRejzner14"} Dütsch-Fredenhagen-Keller-Rejzner 14
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"}> Yang-Mills theory
            ) in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            was then discussed (for trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            and restricted to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariant+observables"} gauge invariant observables
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-complex"} BRST-complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
            in (
            %a{:href => "#FredenhagenRejzner11b"}> Fredenhagen-Rejzner 11b
            ). The generalization of all these constructions from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            to perturbative quantum fields on more general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            (i.e. for more general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravitational
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+fields"} background fields
            such as appearing in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole"} black hole
            physics) was made possible due to the identification of the proper generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+states"} vacuum states
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagators"} Feynman propagators
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+states"} Hadamard states
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"} globally hyperbolic spacetimes
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Radzikowski96"} Radzikowski 96
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} QFT on curved spacetimes
            was developed in a long series of articles by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"} Hollands
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Wald
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"} Brunetti
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Fredenhagen
            and others, now called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+AQFT"} locally covariant perturbative AQFT
          %p
            While this establishes a rigorous construction of perturbative quantum field theory on general gravitational backgrounds, the construction principles had remained somewhat ad-hoc: The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            for the perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            (equivalently for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retarded+products"} retarded products
            of field operators) were well motivated by comparison with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dyson+series"} Dyson series
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            and not the least by their excellent confirmation by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a{:href => "#DuetschFredenhagen01"} Dütsch Fredenhagen 01
            it was observed that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+quantum+field+theory"} free quantum field theory
            are equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+deformation+quantization"} Moyal deformation quantization
            of the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            (the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls+bracket"} Peierls bracket
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of the free field theory (or rather of a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+state"} Hadamard state
            for it) and
            %a{:href => "#Collini16"} Collini 16
            showed that under suitable conditions the perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+observable+algebra"} interacting observable algebra
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov deformation quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of the interacting theory. A general argument to this extent was given in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HawkinsRejzner16"} Hawkins-Rejzner 16
          %p
            This suggests that the construction of the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            ought to be given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 4
            is known. In particular the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenologically
            interesting case of a complete construction of interacting field theories on 4-dimensional spacetimes is presently unknown. For the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            this open problem to go beyond perturbative quantum field theory is one of the “Millennium Problems” (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            this is possibly the
            = succeed "-year" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^4
            = succeed "\u201C)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            does fit into the framework of perturbative QFT, is mathematically well-defined and makes predictions, see the references
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "quantum%20gravity#ReferencesAsAnEffectiveFieldTheory"} there
          %h2#details Details
          %p
            A comprehensive introduction is at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} geometry of physics – perturbative quantum field theory
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization+theory"} main theorem of perturbative renormalization theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"}> renormalization
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radiative+correction"} radiative correction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"}> causal perturbation theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    product in
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
                  %th
                    %math#mathml_27ee5fe71b396df84a9f4b19030bbbae0b7c742f_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,\,
                    induces
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal-ordered+product"} normal-ordered product
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
                    (
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
                    (
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"}> scattering amplitudes
                    )
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retarded+product"} retarded product
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+quantum+observables"} interacting quantum observables
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"} non-perturbative field theory
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The original informal conception of perturbative QFT is due to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"} Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
          %ul
            %li#Dyson49
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freeman+Dyson"}> Freeman Dyson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The raditation theories of Tomonaga, Schwinger and Feynman
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://web.ihep.su/dbserv/compas/src/dyson49b/eng.pdf"} pdf
          %p Non-rigorous but widely used textbooks:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+D.+Bjorken"}> James D. Bjorken
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sidney+D.+Drell"} Sidney D. Drell
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relativistic Quantum Mechanics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/relativisticquan0000bjor/page/n1/mode/2up"} ark:/13960/t5fc2v05h
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://emineter.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/james-d-bjorken-sidney-d-drell-relativistic-quantum-mechanics-1964.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mmmut.ac.in/News_content/14331tpnews_11122020.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+D.+Bjorken"}> James D. Bjorken
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sidney+D.+Drell"} Sidney D. Drell
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relativistic Quantum Fields
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/relativisticquan0000bjor_c5q0"} ark:/13960/t9t22sx2r
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://ivlabs.github.io/resources/physics/books/Relativistic%20Quantum%20Fields%20by%20James%20D.%20Bjorken,%20Sidney%20D.%20Drell.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Peskin"}> Michael Peskin
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Schroeder"} Daniel Schroeder
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1201/9780429503559"} doi:10.1201/9780429503559
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/407703"} spire:407703
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.routledge.com/An-Introduction-To-Quantum-Field-Theory/Peskin-Schroeder/p/book/9780201503975"} ISBN 9780201503975
            %li#Weinberg95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Weinberg"}> Steven Weinberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Quantum Theory of Fields Vol 1: Foundations
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139644167"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781139644167
            %li#Weinberg96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Weinberg"}> Steven Weinberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Quantum Theory of Fields Vol 2: Applications
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139644174"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781139644174
          %p
            The rigorous formulation of renormalized perturbative quantum field theory in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            was first accomplished in
          %ul
            %li#EpsteinGlaser73
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Epstein"}> Henri Epstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Glaser"} Vladimir Glaser
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Role+of+locality+in+perturbation+theory"} The Role of locality in perturbation theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=AIHPA_1973__19_3_211_0"} Numdam
          %p with precursors in
          %ul
            %li#Stueckelberg49
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+St%C3%BCckelberg"}> Ernst Stückelberg
                , D. Rivier, Helv. Phys. Acta, 22 (1949) 215.
            %li#Stueckelberg51
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+St%C3%BCckelberg"}> Ernst Stückelberg
                , J. Green, Helv. Phys. Acta, 24 (1951) 153.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+St%C3%BCckelberg"}> Ernst Stückelberg
                , A. Peterman, ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em La normalisation des constants dans la theorie des quanta
            %li#BogoliubovShirkov59
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolay+Bogoliubov"}> Nikolay Bogoliubov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Shirkov"} Dmitry Shirkov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to the Theory of Quantized Fields
          %p Traditional review includes:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Banks"}> Tom Banks
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modern quantum field theory, A concise introduction
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511811500"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511811500
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://web.phys.ntnu.no/~mika/banks.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthew+Schwartz"}> Matthew Schwartz
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Field Theory and the Standard Model
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/de/universitypress/subjects/physics/theoretical-physics-and-mathematical-physics/quantum-field-theory-and-standard-model?format=HB&isbn=9781107034730"} ISBN:9781107034730
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781139540940"} doi:10.1017/9781139540940
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://assets.cambridge.org/97811070/34730/frontmatter/9781107034730_frontmatter.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michel+Talagrand"}> Michel Talagrand
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What is a Quantum Field Theory? – A first Introduction for Mathematicians
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108225144"} doi:10.1017/9781108225144
          %p
            A seminal compilation of the resulting rigorous understanding of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            is
          %ul
            %li#VeloWightman76
              G. Velo and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+Wightman"} Arthur Wightman
              (eds.):
              = succeed "," do
                %em Renormalization Theory
          %p
            Concrete computations in rigorous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            have been spelled out for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            in
          %ul
            %li#Scharf95
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Finite+Quantum+Electrodynamics+--+The+Causal+Approach"} Finite Quantum Electrodynamics – The Causal Approach
          %p
            and for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+gravity"} perturbative quantum gravity
            in
          %ul
            %li#Scharf01
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantum+Gauge+Theories+--+A+True+Ghost+Story"} Quantum Gauge Theories – A True Ghost Story
          %p
            The treatment of the IR-divergencies by organizing the perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
            was first suggested in
          %ul
            %li#IlinSlavnov78
              V. A. Il’in and D. S. Slavnov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Observable algebras in the S-matrix approach
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/135575"} spire
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01035870"} doi
          %p
            and then developed to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
            in
          %ul
            %li#DuetschFredenhagen01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic Quantum Field Theory, Perturbation Theory, and the Loop Expansion
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0001129"} arXiv:hep-th/0001129
            %li#BrunettiDuetschFredenhagen09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"} Michael Dütsch
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory and the Renormalization Groups
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2038"} arXiv:0901.2038
          %p
            Quantization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"}> Yang-Mills theory
            ) in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            is discussed (for trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            and restricted to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariant+observables"} gauge invariant observables
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-complex"} BRST-complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
            in
          %ul
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in the functional approach to classical field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5112"} arXiv:1101.5112
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5232"} arXiv:1110.5232
          %p and surveyed in:
          %ul
            %li#Rejzner16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"}> Kasia Rejzner
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Perturbative+Algebraic+Quantum+Field+Theory"} Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-25901-7"} doi:10.1007/978-3-319-25901-7
            %li#Schreiber17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} Geometry of Physics – Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/65-409%3A+Mathematical+Quantum+Field+Theory"} Mathematical Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Schreiber_pQFT20211103.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.physicsforums.com/insights/a-first-idea-of-quantum-field-theory/"} PhysicsForums version
            %li#Duetsch18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/From+classical+field+theory+to+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} From classical field theory to perturbative quantum field theory
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-04738-2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-030-04738-2
          %p
            The generalization of all these constructions to quantum fields on general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"} globally hyperbolic spacetimes
            (perturbative
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+states"} Hadamard states
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagators"} Feynman propagators
            in
          %ul
            %li#Radzikowski96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marek+Radzikowski"}> Marek Radzikowski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Micro-local approach to the Hadamard condition in quantum field theory on curved space-time
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104287114"} Euclid
          %p
            and then developed in a long series of articles by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"} Stefan Hollands
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"} Romeo Brunetti
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
            and others. For this see the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
          %p
            The observation that perturbative quantum field theory is equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of the defining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian+density"} local Lagrangian density
            is for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            due to
          %ul
            %li#DuetschFredenhagen01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory and deformation quantization
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://xxx.uni-augsburg.de/abs/hep-th/0101079"} arXiv:hep-th/0101079
            %li#HirschfeldHenselder02
              %p
                A. C. Hirshfeld, P. Henselder,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Star Products and Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0208194"} arXiv:hep-th/0208194
          %p
            and for interacting field theories (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"}> causal perturbation theory
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"}> perturbative AQFT
            ) due
          %ul
            %li#Collini16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Collini"}> Giovanni Collini
                ,
                %em Fedosov Quantization and Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09626"}> arXiv:1603.09626
                )
            %li#HawkinsRejzner16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eli+Hawkins"}> Eli Hawkins
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                %em The Star Product in Interacting Quantum Field Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.09157"}> arXiv:1612.09157
                )
          %p
            For more see the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
          %p
            The relation of the construction via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            was understood in
          %ul
            %li#GarciaBondiaLazzarini00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jose+Gracia-Bondia"}> Jose Gracia-Bondia
                , S. Lazzarini,
                %em Connes-Kreimer-Epstein-Glaser Renormalization
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0006106"}> arXiv:hep-th/0006106
                )
            %li#Keller10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kai+Keller"}> Kai Keller
                , chapter IV of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dimensional Regularization in Position Space and a Forest Formula for Regularized Epstein-Glaser Renormalization
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2148"} arXxiv:1006.2148
            %li#DuetschFredenhagenKellerRejzner14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kai+Keller"} Kai Keller
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dimensional Regularization in Position Space, and a Forest Formula for Epstein-Glaser Renormalization
              %p
                55(12), 122303 (2014) (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.5424"}> arXiv:1311.5424
                )
          %p (…)
          %h3#ReferencesNonConvergenceOfThePerturbationSeries Non-convergence of the perturbation series
          %p
            The argument that the perturbation series of realistic pQFTs necessarily
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergent+series"} diverges
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
            (is at best an
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic series
          %ul
            %li#Dyson52
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freeman+Dyson"}> Freeman Dyson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Divergence of perturbation theory in quantum electrodynamics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/29799?ln=en"} spire
          %p and is made more precise in
          %ul
            %li#Lipatov77
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lev+Lipatov"}> Lev Lipatov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Divergence of the Perturbation Theory Series and the Quasiclassical Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://jetp.ac.ru/cgi-bin/dn/e_045_02_0216.pdf"} pdf
          %p recalled for instance in
          %ul
            %li#Suslov05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Suslov"}> Igor Suslov
                , section 1 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Divergent perturbation series
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0510142"} arXiv:hep-ph/0510142
            %li
              %p
                Justin Bond, last section of
                %em Perturbative QFT is Asymptotic; is Divergent; is Problematic in Principle
                (
                %a{:href => "https://mcgreevy.physics.ucsd.edu/s13/final-papers/2013S-215C-Bond-Justin.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#FloryHellingSluka12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mario+Flory"}> Mario Flory
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+C.+Helling"} Robert C. Helling
                %em How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love QFT
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.2714"}> arXiv:1201.2714
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Strocchi13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franco+Strocchi"}> Franco Strocchi
                , §2.2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to Non-Perturbative Foundations of Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199671571.001.0001"} doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199671571.001.0001
            %li#HollandsWald14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum fields in curved spacetime
                %strong 574
                (2015) 1-35 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.2026"}> arXiv:1401.2026
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physrep.2015.02.001"} doi:10.1016/j.physrep.2015.02.001
            %li
              %p
                Marco Serone, from 2:46 on in
                %em
                  A look at
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mn 2
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^4_2
                  using perturbation theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J4nxvY1rOhI"}> recording
                )
          %p#ReferencesForInverseOfCouplingArgument
            The argument that the perturbation series should be trustworthy for number of terms smaller than the inverse of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            is recalled in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FloryHellingSluka12"} Flory, Helling &amp; Sluka 2012, p. 8 &amp; eq. (34) &amp; Sec. 2.5
          %p Exposition also in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+Schwichtenberg"}> Jakob Schwichtenberg
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://jakobschwichtenberg.com/divergence-perturbation-series-qft"} divergence-perturbation-series-qft
          %p
            For the example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^4
              \-theory
            this non-convergence of the perturbation series is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Helling
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+C.+Helling"}> Robert C. Helling
                , p. 4 of
                %em Solving classical field equations
                (
                %a{:href => "http://homepages.physik.uni-muenchen.de/~helling/classical_fields.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/HellingClassicalQFT.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#BakulevShirkov10
              %p
                Alexander P. Bakulev,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Shirkov"} Dmitry Shirkov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Inevitability and Importance of Non-Perturbative Elements in Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.2380"} arXiv:1102.2380
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Carl M. Bender, Carlo Heissenberg,
              %em Convergent and Divergent Series in Physics
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.05164"}> arXiv:1703.05164
              )
          %p
            And see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
              –
              %a{:href => "perturbation+theory#ReferencesDivergenceConvergence"} On divergence/convergence
          %p#ReferencesBreakdownAtLargeNumberOfInsertions
            Discussion of further issues, even when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resummation"} resummation
            is thought to apply, arising for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            at large
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (large number of external particles in a scattering process):
          %p
            failure of unitarity (for
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5En+interaction"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^n
                \-theory
          %ul
            %li
              Sebastian Schenk,
              %em The Breakdown of Resummed Perturbation Theory at High Energies
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.00549"}> arXiv:2109.00549
              )
          %p
            failure of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locality"} locality
            (for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+gravity"} perturbative quantum gravity
            and
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sudip+Ghosh"}> Sudip Ghosh
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Suvrat+Raju"} Suvrat Raju
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Loss of locality in gravitational correlators with a large number of insertions
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07424"} arXiv:1706.07424
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sudip+Ghosh"}> Sudip Ghosh
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Suvrat+Raju"} Suvrat Raju
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Breakdown of String Perturbation Theory for Many External Particles
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.08003"} arXiv:1611.08003
          %h3#linfinity_algebra_structure L-infinity algebra structure
          %p
            Further identification of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"} L-infinity algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            in the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %ul
            %li#Froeb18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markus+Fr%C3%B6b"}> Markus Fröb
                ,
                %em Anomalies in time-ordered products and applications to the BV-BRST formulation of quantum gauge theories
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.10235"}> arXiv:1803.10235
                )
            %li#Arvanitakis19
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Arvanitakis"}> Alex Arvanitakis
                ,
                %em
                  The
                  = succeed "-algebra" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi L
                            %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.05643"}> arXiv:1903.05643
                )
        .revisedby
          %p
